<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=prosthesis">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prosthesis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3000–2800 BCE</span> · Also known as prosthetic implant</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A prosthesis is an artificial device that replaces a missing body part lost through injury, disease or a condition present at birth. The earliest archaeological evidence is an eye prosthesis, probably of bitumen paste covered with a thin layer of gold, buried with a woman at Shahr-i Shōkhta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> about 3000 to 2800 BCE. A wood and leather artificial big toe found on a mummy near Thebes, dated 950 to 710 BCE, shows signs of use. Ambroise Paré, considered the father of modern prosthetics, designed an above-knee device, and functional prostheses appeared in the 1500s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prosthesis.jpg" alt="Prosthesis" width="330" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The U.S. Army · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flickr_-_The_U.S._Army_-_U.S._Army_World_Class_Athlete_Program_Paralympic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 3000–2800 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Shahr-i Shōkhta, Iran</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The wood and leather toe from a burial near Thebes, dated 950 to 710 BCE, is described as the first confirmed use of a prosthetic device, as it shows wear; before its discovery in 2000 the oldest known was an artificial leg from Capua.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=prosthesis">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
